WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.500
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.349
In many previous episodes, we talked about
generative adversarial networks, a recent

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:14.889
new line in machine learning research with
some absolutely fantastic results in a variety

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:15.889
of areas.

00:00:15.889 --> 00:00:21.759
They can synthesize new images of animals,
create 3d models from photos, or dream up

00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:24.730
new products based on our edits of an image.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.460
A generative adversarial network means that
we have two neural networks battling each

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.189
other in an arms race.

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:36.149
The generator network tries to create more
and more realistic images, and these are passed

00:00:36.149 --> 00:00:41.329
to the discriminator network which tries to
learn the difference between real photographs

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:43.470
and fake, forged images.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:48.410
During this process, the two neural networks
learn and improve together until they become

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.550
experts at their own craft.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.239
And as you can see, the results are fantastic.

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:58.610
However, training these networks against each
other is anything but roses and sunshine.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:03.530
We don't know if the process converges or
if we reach Nash equilibrium.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:08.800
Nash equilibrium is a state where both actors
believe they have found an optimal strategy

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.550
while taking into account the other actor's
possible decisions, and neither of them have

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.380
interest in changing their strategy.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.490
This is a classical scenario in game theory
where two convicted criminals are pondering

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:26.290
whether they should snitch on each other without
knowing how the other decided to act.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:30.920
If you wish to hear more about the Nash-equilibrium,
I've a put a link to Khan Academy's video

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.940
in the description, make sure to check it
out, you'll love it!

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:40.170
I find it highly exciting that there are parallels
in AI and game theory, however, the even cooler

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:45.860
thing is that here, we try to build a system
where we don't have to deal with such a situation.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:51.440
This is called Generative Latent Optimization,
GLO in short and it is about introducing tricks

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.970
to do this by only using a generator network.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:59.940
If you have ever read up on font design, you
know that it is a highly complex field.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:04.490
However, if we'd like to create a new font
type, what we're typically interested in is

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:09.679
only a few features, like how curvy they are,
or whether we're dealing with a serif kind

00:02:09.679 --> 00:02:12.469
of font, and simple descriptions like that.

00:02:12.469 --> 00:02:18.269
The same principle can be applied to human
faces, animals, and most topics you can imagine.

00:02:18.269 --> 00:02:23.340
This means that there are many complex concepts
that contain a ton of information, most of

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.849
which can be captured by a simple description
with only a few features.

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:33.870
This is done by projecting this high-dimensional
data onto a low-dimensional latent space.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:39.239
This latent space helps eliminating adversarial
optimization, which makes this system much

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:44.760
easier to train, and the main selling point
is that it still retains the attractive properties

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.230
of generative adversarial networks.

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:51.480
This means that it can synthesize new samples
from the learned dataset.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:59.359
If it had learned the concept of birds, it
will be able to synthesize new bird species.

00:02:59.359 --> 00:03:03.360
It can perform continuous interpolation between
data points.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:08.450
This means that for instance, we can produce
intermediate states between two chosen furniture

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:10.400
types or light fixtures.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:17.370
It is also able to perform simple arithmetic
operations between any number of data points.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:24.299
For instance, if A is males with sunglasses,
B are males without sunglasses, and C are

00:03:24.299 --> 00:03:30.569
females, then A-B+C is going to generate females
in sunglasses.

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:34.980
It can also do super resolution and much,
much more, make sure to have a look at the

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.909
paper in the video description.

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:42.120
Now, before we go, we shall address the elephant
in the room: these images are tiny.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.659
Our seasoned Fellow Scholars know that for
generative adversarial networks, there are

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:51.840
plenty of works on how to synthesize high
resolution images with more details.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.750
This means that this is a piece of work that
opens up exciting new horizons, but it is

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:01.829
not to be measured against the tenth followup
work on top of a more established line of

00:04:01.829 --> 00:04:02.829
research.

00:04:02.829 --> 00:04:06.959
Two Minute Papers will be here for you to
keep you updated on the progress, which is,

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:10.799
as we know, staggeringly quick in machine
learning research.

00:04:10.799 --> 00:04:14.709
Don't forget to subscribe and click the bell
icon to never miss an episode.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

